aine diagnosis and treatment in China and was widely referenced by medical departments. The 2022 guideline builds upon this earlier work, incorporating new evidence and updated clinical practices. The continuity between the two versions reflects a sustained effort to improve patient care and standardize treatment protocols over time. The 2016 guideline remains relevant as a historical reference point and a basis for comparison in evaluating the progress of clinical practice. [Data: Entities (396), Relationships (465, 466, 467, 468)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"2016 guideline as the foundation for the 2022 update\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The neurology department is a primary target audience for the migraine diagnosis and treatment guidelines. As a specialized clinical unit responsible for diagnosing and managing neurological disorders, it plays a central role in implementing the guideline recommendations. The guideline’s explicit reference to neurologists ensures that the most specialized practitioners are equipped with standardized protocols for treating migraine. This focus enhances the quality and consistency of care for patients with complex neurological conditions. [Data: Entities (397), Relationships (465)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Neurology department as a primary target for guideline implementation\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Pain management departments and general practice departments are also key target audiences for the migraine guidelines. The inclusion of pain specialists (pain department) acknowledges the significant role of chronic pain in migraine patients, while the reference to general practitioners and internal medicine physicians reflects the need for early diagnosis and management in primary care settings. This broad targeting ensures that the guideline reaches a wide range of healthcare providers who may encounter migraine patients in various clinical contexts. The integration